#{[{:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139365} :activity/id #uuid "2320ac0f-1ba4-5fab-8b23-0557556abfdc" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050076 :topic/id #uuid "2b568414-117a-54db-9c62-047f1acb1909" :topic/math-academy-id 2230} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139365 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139596 :tutorial/content "The formula for the speed $S$ of a moving object is\n\n$$\nS=\\dfrac{D}{T}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is the distance covered, and $T$ is the time taken.\n\nFor example, if a car travels a distance of $600\\,\\textrm{m}$ within a time of $30\\,\\textrm{s}$, at constant speed, then we can find the speed of the car using the formula as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS &= \\frac{D}{T} \\\\\n&= \\frac{600}{30} \\\\\n&= 20\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBut what is the unit of speed? We can find it by substituting the units for distance and time into the formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nS &= \\frac{D}{T} \\\\\n&= \\frac{meters}{seconds} \\\\\n&= \\frac{m}{s}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the speed of the car is $20\\,\\textrm{m}/\\textrm{s}$. When the unit has a quotient like this, we say \"meters *per* second.\"" :tutorial/id #uuid "61193d89-1b00-531f-be9f-18801cc08970" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1399 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "2c2e40d2-0cf6-5448-9925-a2a8ae44cc69" :step/math-academy-id 3018 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139367}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139367 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138437 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138310 :question/id #uuid "36127b89-97ba-5f98-bd8e-b36ae1a261f4" :question/math-academy-id "e-896"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e447dc49-3549-568c-ade5-86601beeb3a6" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049253} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051057}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138795} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138797}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Units When There Is No Cancellation"} :step/id #uuid "2bfae739-4b1c-551e-8e72-be87c28b1bef" :step/math-academy-id 3019 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139368}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139368 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138438 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138311 :question/id #uuid "ee3fa65e-1146-598a-a89d-33d287e88066" :question/math-academy-id "e-897"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "4e1b4e78-8b4a-59ea-8fac-9092c2eeb67a" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050843} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051029}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138793} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139082}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Units When There Is Cancellation"} :step/id #uuid "82c13ded-0a7f-5183-b153-dbfb81baff87" :step/math-academy-id 3022 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139369}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139369 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139597 :tutorial/content "Sometimes, we can be given a formula with values whose units are not the standard ones we know.\n\nFor instance, suppose the formula for a person's popularity $P$ is given by\n\n$$\nP=\\dfrac{2f}{s}\n$$\n\nwhere $f$ is the number of friends that person has, and $s$ is the number of siblings they have.\n\nIf we want to find the unit of popularity, then we need to substitute the units for the number of friends ($f$) and the number of siblings ($s$) into the formula.\n\n- The units for the number of friends is '$\\textrm{friends}$' since we say 'I have $5$ friends'.\n- Similarly, the units for the number of siblings is '$\\textrm{siblings}$.'\n\nSubstituting these units into the formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP &= \\frac{2f}{s} \\\\\n&= \\frac{2 \\cdot friends}{siblings} \\\\\n&= 2 \\cdot \\frac{friends}{siblings}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the appropriate unit is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{friends}}{\\textrm{siblings}}\n$$\n\n, or \"friends per sibling.\" Here we ignore the constant $2$ since it does not affect the units." :tutorial/id #uuid "ab30765e-d9e3-53d5-a050-8bd889054974" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1400 :tutorial/title "Formulas With Non-Standard Units"} :step/id #uuid "976f91da-bd0c-5ab2-bca4-a77eb3665217" :step/math-academy-id 3024 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139370}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139370 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138439 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138312 :question/id #uuid "b0212d4c-eb7a-5cec-8eb1-1c60be355f4e" :question/math-academy-id "e-898"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "deb16829-8ec8-5cbb-b81f-2c526bce165e" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138796} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186139083}] :knowledge-point/title "Dividing Nonstandard Units"} :step/id #uuid "ecd1aeef-36e3-51c0-8e9f-f64384ff00d8" :step/math-academy-id 3025}] :activity/title "Determining Units in Formulas" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186138590}]}
